Enable assertions in the test build.

This is NOT one of the defect fixes. It is the oracle the suite needs, and it is
applied in BOTH red and green modes so that the only difference between them is
the fixes themselves.

Upstream builds without --enable-cassert: project.yaml lists only the address
sanitizer, and build.sh runs a bare ../configure twice. That is precisely why
these defects survived for years. PostgreSQL has thousands of internal
consistency checks that are compiled out unless asked for, and without them a
harness that hands the wrong structure to a function does not crash -- it
quietly does the wrong thing and keeps producing output that looks like work. A
low bug count then reads as "the database is clean" rather than "nothing is
being tested".

So four of the six cases are unobservable without this: parses-sql,
recovers-on-error, keeps-context and temp-relation all describe a harness
corrupting state rather than crashing. With assertions on they abort on the
first input; with them off they proceed and produce nothing to assert against.

The suite therefore turns assertions on and leaves everything else exactly as
upstream ships it. That doubles as a demonstration of the recommendation worth
making independently of any individual fix: build with assertions periodically,
because it is the cheapest bug detector in the tree and it is currently off.

--- a/projects/postgresql/build.sh
+++ b/projects/postgresql/build.sh
@@ -41,11 +41,11 @@
 # Using the system ICU keeps headers, shared libraries and static libraries all
 # at the same version, and drops a network fetch plus a full ICU build.

-CC="" CXX="" CFLAGS="" CXXFLAGS="" su fuzzuser -c ../configure
+CC="" CXX="" CFLAGS="" CXXFLAGS="" su fuzzuser -c "../configure --enable-cassert"
 cd src/backend/fuzzer
 su fuzzuser -c "make -j10 createdb"
 chown -R root .
 mv temp/data .
 cp -r data $OUT/
 cd ../../..
 cp -r tmp_install $OUT/
 make clean

-../configure
+../configure --enable-cassert
 make -j$(nproc)
